{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-08-14T11:23:39.049Z","session_id":"ses_ffffca67dffeTUkRgN9Nv5Ysdx","name":"write","input":{"content":"# Reviewed self-development release checklist\n\nUse this checklist before a Formal AI release is versioned or tagged. Each\nitem protects the reviewed, session-backed contribution loop required by E77.\n\n## 1. Refresh the release boundary\n\n- Fetch `origin/main` before measuring the candidate branch.\n- Fetch annotated tags so the latest release boundary exists locally.\n- Confirm the newest ledger row names the newest fetched release tag.\n- Resolve the tag to a commit instead of trusting a local branch name.\n- Measure the exact `<latest-tag>..HEAD` range used by release automation.\n- Stop if the checkout cannot prove which tag begins the release cycle.\n\n## 2. Identify the authored contribution\n\n- Require at least one non-merge commit with a Formal AI session trailer.\n- Require the matching evidence trailer in the same commit message.\n- Require a canonical GitHub pull-request URL in the PR trailer.\n- Reject partial trailer sets instead of guessing the missing identity.\n- Keep captured logs separate from the changed-line authorship calculation.\n- Verify the committed evidence names both Formal AI and the session id.\n\n## 3. Prove ordinary pull-request review\n\n- Walk first-parent merge history for the release range.\n- Match the pull-request number in the merge subject to the trailer URL.\n- Inspect the merge commit's second parent as the reviewed branch tip.\n- Require the attributed commit object on that second-parent branch.\n- Require the same object to be absent from the merge's first parent.\n- Reject a direct commit that merely claims a pull-request URL.\n\n## 4. Project the non-decreasing target\n\n- Read only ledger rows produced by the current metric definition.\n- Use the configured trailing release window without mixing epochs.\n- Project the candidate release without writing a ledger row.\n- Compare the candidate projection with the durable release target.\n- Compare PR-time impact with the no-op base projection.\n- Add real session-backed work when the candidate would lower the share.\n\n## 5. Keep retries idempotent\n\n- Exclude an existing row for the tag from its own retry projection.\n- Accept a repeated release record only when every field is identical.\n- Reject duplicate tag rows that disagree with committed history.\n- Leave version files unchanged until eligibility succeeds.\n- Re-run the same precondition after fetching newer tags or main commits.\n- Preserve the open release range when more reviewed work is required.\n\n## 6. Preserve existing trust gates\n\n- Do not grant Formal AI merge, approval, or promotion authority.\n- Send authored bytes through ordinary human review.\n- Require the same CI matrix used for other repository changes.\n- Keep trusted promotion rules in force for learned runtime behavior.\n- Treat the release gate as evidence verification, not authorization.\n- Record failures without bypassing branch protection.\n\n## 7. Validate before version mutation\n\n- Run the self-development release gate before editing Cargo metadata.\n- Confirm a qualifying reviewed PR exists in the current release range.\n- Confirm the projected share meets the non-decreasing target.\n- Confirm the working tree is clean before automated version changes.\n- Abort without partial edits when any precondition fails.\n- Re-run after remediation instead of increasing a timeout or threshold.\n\n## 8. Record the successful release\n\n- Store the release tag, range endpoints, and canonical until commit.\n- Store authored and total changed-line counts.\n- Store authored and total non-merge commit counts.\n- Store the per-release and trailing percentages.\n- Store the target that governed the release.\n- Store every qualifying Formal AI-authored pull-request URL.\n\n## 9. Publish reviewable evidence\n\n- Include the self-hosting share in generated release notes.\n- Include the trailing share and non-decreasing target.\n- Link the reviewed pull requests that satisfied the release floor.\n- Preserve raw Agent CLI output for replay and audit.\n- Preserve the Formal AI server trace for the recorded session.\n- Keep the exact authored artifact beside its evidence bundle.\n\n## 10. Investigate a red ratchet\n\n- Verify the run used the newest commit and release tag.\n- Compare timestamps and head SHA before diagnosing stale CI data.\n- Preserve the complete failed workflow log locally.\n- Find the exact numerator and denominator behind both projections.\n- Attribute only work actually produced by a recorded Formal AI session.\n- Split unrelated work instead of weakening the target.\n\n## 11. Finalize the pull request\n\n- Merge the latest default branch and resolve generated-file drift.\n- Run focused regressions before the full local CI-equivalent suite.\n- Verify strict lint, examples, policies, and derived artifacts.\n- Push only the prepared issue branch.\n- Confirm every required check belongs to the pushed head SHA.\n- Mark the pull request ready only after all required checks pass.\n\nCompletion means the release contains real Formal AI work whose unchanged\nbytes passed normal review, CI, promotion rules, and the self-hosting ratchet.","filePath":"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1786670765158/docs/case-studies/issue-924/v0343-ratchet-authorship/release-checklist.md"},"tool_use_id":"prt_000036aa7001EGZwEGDJGNnO3m"}
